{"machine": "Majestic class", "source_fingerprint": "210ce48cc96241bcf7be4c48bd1db6f0ccff3d3219ac02bf3429a6d27030edc0", "result": {"paragraph": "Majestic class light fleet carriers were part of the 1942 Design Light Fleet Carrier programme, the most ambitious of the Royal Navy in WW2. They represented a second wave of constructions, identical but with modifications, notably a strengthened flight deck. The Admiralty reclassified them as the Majestic class according to the lead ship in September 1945. Only 5 Majestic class ships were completed, the rest being either converted postwar to a new design (Centaur class) or scrapped. Of the Majestic class, apart from HMS Leviathan which was cancelled, all five served postwar with the RCN, RAN, and Indian Navy, not with the Royal Navy.", "word_count": 104, "passed": true, "warnings": [], "claim_map": [{"sentence": "Majestic class light fleet carriers were part of the 1942 Design Light Fleet Carrier programme, the most ambitious of the Royal Navy in WW2.", "citations": [{"excerpt_id": "S6-E1", "source_id": "S6", "source_title": "Majestic class aircraft carriers - naval encyclopedia", "source_url": "https://naval-encyclopedia.com/cold-war/uk/majestic-class-aircraft-carriers.php", "locator": "S6-E1; query=\"Majestic class\" class displacement armament beam launched design tradeoff limitation compromise", "quote": "Majestic class aircraft carriers \u2013 naval encyclopedia Skip to content United Kingdom: 5 light fleet aircraft carriers: HMS Majestic, Terrible, Powerful, Magnificient, Hercules, Powerful, 1 more cancelled (1944-1999) British Cold War Carriers: Majestic class | Centaur class | HMS Victorious | HMS Eagle | HMS Ark Royal | HMS Hermes | CVA-01 project | Invincible class | HMS Argus | Queen Elisabeth class (planned) The majestic class were part of the 1942 Design Light Fleet Carrier programme, the most ambitious of the Royal Navy in WW2.", "source_capture_method": "fetched_page"}]}, {"sentence": "They represented a sec